> > I'd say balancing the artifacts would make them more interesting.  If
> > I had to arbitrarily change (non-quest) artifacts to make some more
> > appealing (and others less appealing), here are some ideas:
> 
> Idea:
> *banes can be #invoked to cause fear/confusion in all monsters in
> sight.  (Come to think of it, fear and confusion might make good
> wizard spells). Probably not demonband or dragonbane.

That's a thought.  Though, invocation seems to be relegated to quest
artifacts, as it is.

> Were the powers of artifacts mostly set before weapon skills?  I think
> weapon skills tend to increase the power of the mainstream weapon
> types and decrease the power of the fringe ones, especially those
> with highly specific use like Trollsbane.  I think the damage of most
> banes should be increased to x3 at least, since most people using
> them will be doing so at a -4/-2 or after burning a skill slot.

That's an interesting point.  That's partially why I thought an
instakilling would be better.  I think the problem isn't that the
banes don't do enough damage against their specific enemies, but
instead that they aren't good enough of a weapon in general to benefit
keeping them around or blowing skill slots on.

Right now, since the damage against their bane is doubled, you have to
blow a few enchant scrolls just to get it to be useful and blow a
skill slot.  That's why I thought insta-killing would make them useful
in the early game where you don't have enchant scrolls.  Other than
demons, the enemies that the banes are geared towards are a problem
only in the early game.
 
> > Grayswandir: 1.5x damage instead of double
> 
> Bringing down the top doesn't seem right for some reason.
> I think grayswandir could use a slight and subtle nerfing, but
> that's a different thread...

Someone else suggested 1.75x instead.  My reasoning for bringing down
the top is this: If one weapon is too powerful (3x against everything?
insta-kills everything? Not that this would be a fun weapon, but just
hypothetically...), then most everyone will want to use it and will
consider all the other artifacts even less.  A nigh-assured way to win
would be to sac until you got it.  Maybe that's an extreme, but I
think bringing down the top puts everything else in a better light.

Right now, Grayswandir does more average damage to demons at than
Demonbane does at the same enchantment (as a result of the silver, I
think).  Maybe it's just me, but that seems really wrong.

> > Trollsbane: kills trolls instantly (or trolls hit/killed by it don't
> > regen?)
> 
> I like no ressies like everyone else. I think if it kills them they should
> leave no corpse, either, with some satisfactory "the troll disappears
> into noxious fumes" type message.

That sounds like a great idea.  It would work great with the other
insta-kill weapons too.  No corpses means less food and fewer
intrinsics (in the case of dragons or giants).

> > Werebane: werefoo cannot summon help when wielded
> 
> Immunity to lycanthropy?   Perhaps it should also force them
> back into human form when it hits them.

Werebane already confers the immunity.  I think it would be more
interesting to force them back into human form when being hit though,
rather than just forcing them out of it when being wielded or
something like that.
 
> > Ogresmasher: kills ogres instantly
> 
> Probably too strong, but on the other hand, sure why not.
 
I figure ogres are really a special case of enemies.  It's not really
that overpowered considering how few ogres you tend to meet.  It's
only +d5 to hit other monsters, so it's not that great of a general
purpose weapon.

> > Demonbane: does 2X damage against riders, as well (or more??)
> 
> 2x vs. riders, 3x versus other &s?

3x against all & seems a little too much.  2.5x maybe? On the other
hand, I'm not sure what else could be added to be more useful.  I like
the idea of 2x and a chance to stun demons.

> > Tsurugi: always insta-kill engulfers
> 
> Including air elementals on their plane?

You think that's too unbalanced? Perhaps a larger chance of bisection
or something else interesting would be better.  At the very least a
higher damage bonus is needed.  As a samurai quest artifact weapon,
you'd think that it would actually /be/ a weapon that would be worth
using.

Randomly, I've always liked the idea of an artifact acting as an
amulet of life saving, but I don't know if the Tsurugi is the right
place to do that.  Maybe it's such a good weapon that it saves your
life in battle (once) if wielded, reverting afterwards to a normal
tsurugi (with the same rustproof, buc, and enchant status).

> > Grimtooth (I forget this even exists sometimes): always poisoned
> 
> (Not sure what poisoning does to monsters...)

As far as I can remember, I think it's a chance to insta-kill them. 
There's no such analogy to losing strength like player poisoning,
though I think it should.

> > Orcrist: 3X damage to orcs instead
> 
> Seems a good candidate for invoking for fear, although I'm not
> sure orcs are ever a problem by the time most people get their
> first artifact.

Maybe just wielding it would cause fear in orcs (with some random
small chance).  It would certainly make some sense for orcs to run
away from something like Orcrist.

> > [Giantslayer]
